[00:36.12]Conversation One
[00:38.71]W: Welcome to Work Place. 
[00:40.64] And in today's program, we are looking at the results of two recently published surveys, which both deal with the same topic¡ªHappiness at Work. 
[00:50.23] John, tell us about the first survey. 
[00:53.22]M: Well, this was done by a human resources consultancy who interviewed more than 1,000 workers and established a top 10 of the factors which make people happy at work. 
[01:06.02] The most important factor, for the majority of the people interviewed, was having friendly, supportive colleagues. 
[01:13.78] In fact, 73% of people interviewed put their relationship with colleagues as the key factor contributing to happiness at work, which is a very high percentage. 
[01:25.46] The second most important factor was having work that is enjoyable. 
[01:29.83] The two least important factors were having one's achievements recognized and rather surprisingly,earning a competitive salary. 
[01:39.45]W: So we are not mainly motivated by money?
[01:42.77]M: Apparently not. 
[01:44.12]W: Any other interesting information in the survey?
[01:47.23]M: Yes. For example, 25% of the working people interviewed described themselves as very happy at work. 
[01:56.00] However, 20% of employees described themselves as being unhappy. 
[02:02.03]W: That's quite a lot of unhappy people at work every day. 
[02:06.34]M: It is, isn't it? 
[02:07.84]And there were several more interesting conclusions revealed by the survey. 
[02:13.22] First of all, small is beautiful. 
[02:15.80] People definitely prefer working for smaller organizations or companies with less than 100 staff. 
[02:22.74] We also find out that, generally speaking, women were happier in their work than men. 
[02:28.71] W: Yes, we are, aren't we?
[02:30.95]M: And workers on part-time contracts who only work 4 or 5 hours a day are happier than those who work full-time. 
[02:39.36] The researchers concluded that this is probably due to a better work-life balance. 
[02:44.64]W: Are bosses happier than their employees?
[02:47.57]M: Yes. Perhaps not surprisingly, the higher people go in a company, the happier they are. 
[02:53.64] So senior managers enjoy their jobs more than people working under them. 
[02:58.68]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard. 
[03:04.90]1. What is the number one factor that made employees happy according to the survey?
[03:26.06]2. What is the percentage of the people surveyed who felt unhappy at work?
[03:46.10]3. What kind of companies are popular with employees?
[04:04.29]4.  What is the possible reason for people on part-time contracts to be happier?
[04:22.88]Conversation Two
[04:25.86]W: Mr. De Keyzer, I am a great lover of your book, Moments Before the Flood. 
[04:32.38] Can you tell us how you first became interested in the subject matter?
[04:37.20]M: In 2006, when the concert hall of the city of Bruges asked me to take some pictures for a catalogue for a new concert season around the theme of water, I found myself working along the Belgian coastline. 
[04:51.68] As there had been numerous alarming articles in the press about a climate catastrophe waiting to happen, I started looking at the sea and the beach very differently, a place where I spent so many perfect days as a child. 
[05:07.07] This fear of a looming danger became the subject of a large-scale photo project. 
[05:13.22] W: You wrote in the book ¡°I don't want to photograph the disaster; I want to photograph the disaster waiting to happen. ¡± 
[05:22.00]Can you talk a bit about that?
[05:24.06]M: It is clear now that it's a matter of time before the entire European coastline disappears under water. 
[05:31.94] The same goes for numerous big cities around the world. 
[05:36.09] My idea was to photograph this beautiful and very unique coastline, reaching history, before it's too late¡ªas a last witness. 
[05:46.78]W: Can you talk a bit about how history plays a role in this project?
[05:51.29]M: Sure. The project is also about the history of Europe looking at the sea and wondering when the next enemy would appear. 
[06:00.06] In the images, you see all kinds of possible defense constructions to hold back the Romans, Germans, Vikings, and now nature as enemy No. 1. 
[06:13.26] For example, there is the image of the bridge into the sea taken at the Normandy D-Day landing site. 
[06:20.71] Also, Venice, the city eternally threatened by the sea, where every morning wooden pathways have to be set up to allow tourists to reach their hotels. 
[06:31.25]W: Thank you, Mr. De Keyzer. 
[06:32.89] It was a pleasure to have you with us today. 
[06:36.31]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard. 
[06:41.56]5. What does the man say about the book Moments Before the Flood?
[07:00.53]6. When did the man get his idea for the work?
[07:18.11]7. What will happen when the climate catastrophe occurs? 
[07:36.42]8. What does the man say about Venice?
