[08:24.59]Passage One
[08:26.22]Most Americans don't eat enough fruits, vegetables or whole grains. 
[08:31.77] Research now says adding fiber to the teen diet may help lower the risk of breast cancer. 
[08:38.88] Conversations about the benefits of fiber are probably more common in nursing homes than high schools. 
[08:46.92] But along comes a new study that could change that. 
[08:50.85] Kristi King, a diet specialist at Texas Children's Hospital, finds it hard to get teenage patients' attention about healthy eating by telling them that eating lots of high-fiber foods could reduce the risk of breast cancer before middle age. 
[09:08.70] That's a powerful message. 
[09:11.07] The new finding is based on a study of 44, 000 women. 
[09:15.78] They were surveyed about their diets during high school and their eating habits were tracked for two decades. 
[09:22.38] It turns out that those who consumed the highest levels of fiber during adolescence had a lower risk of developing breast cancer, compared to the women who ate the least fiber. 
[09:34.47] This important study demonstrates that the more fiber you eat during your high school years, the lower your risk is in developing breast cancer in later life. 
[09:45.09] The finding points to long-standing evidence that fiber may reduce circulating female hormone levels, which could explain the reduced risk. 
[09:55.07] The bottom line here is the more fiber you eat,perhaps, a lower level of hormone in your body, and therefore, a lower lifetime risk of developing breast cancer. 
[10:06.66] High-fiber diets are also linked to a reduced risk of heart disease and diabetes. 
[10:12.53] That's why women are told to eat 25 grams of fiber a day¡ªmen even more. 
[10:19.35]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard. 
[10:25.32]9. What does the new study tell about adding fiber to the teen diet?
[10:43.94]10. What do we learn about the survey of the 44,000 women?
[11:03.03]11. What explanation does the speaker offer for the research finding?
[11:23.55]Passage Two
[11:25.37]When facing a new situation, some people tend to rehearse their defeat by spending too much time anticipating the worst. 
[11:33.68] I remember talking with a young lawyer who was about to begin her first jury trial. 
[11:39.76] She was very nervous. 
[11:41.38] I asked what impression she wanted to make on the jury. 
[11:45.74] She replied, ¡°I don't want to look too inexperienced; I don't want them to suspect this is my first trial.¡± 
[11:53.90]This lawyer had fallen victim to the ¡°don'ts¡± syndrome, a form of negative goal setting. 
[12:00.70] The ¡°don'ts¡± can be self-fulfilling because your mind responds to pictures. 
[12:05.68] Research conducted at Stanford University shows a mental image fires the nervous system the same way as actually doing something. 
[12:14.96] That means when a golfer tells himself ¡°Don't hit the ball into the water,¡±his mind sees the image of the ball flying into the water. 
[12:24.75] So guess where the ball will go. 
[12:27.67] Consequently, before going into any stressful situation, focus only on what you want to have happen. 
[12:35.07] I asked the lawyer again how she wanted to appear at her first trial, and this time she said, ¡°I want to look professional and self-assured.¡± 
[12:45.30]I told her to create a picture of what ¡°self-assured¡± would look like. 
[12:50.29] To her it meant moving confidently around the court room, using convincing body language and projecting her voice so it could be heard from the judge's bench to the back door. 
[13:02.74] She also imagined a skillful closing argument and a winning trial. 
[13:07.96] A few weeks after this positive dress rehearsal, the young lawyer did win. 
[13:14.32]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard. 
[13:20.59]12. What do some people do when they face a new situation?
[13:39.32]13. What does the research conducted at Stanford University show?
[13:57.55]14. What advice does the speaker give to people in a stressful situation?
[14:17.00]15. What do we learn about the lawyer in the court?
